Subject: [PATCH 06/13] hls: hand out the live playlist after the first segment

Intent: GetLiveHlsStream (Videos/{itemId}/live.m3u8, the segmented live
path) waited for the requested MinSegments (2 or 3 when the client
sends none) before returning the playlist of a transcode it had just
started, so playback start paid one or two extra segment durations
while ffmpeg was already producing. Return it once ONE segment exists
instead: a growing playlist is ordinary HLS, and hls.js (web, webOS)
and ExoPlayer (Fire TV) poll it and apply their own buffering either
way. Only the first response of a newly started transcode changes;
the VOD master/main playlists are untouched. Carried unchanged onto
12.x (same anchor, three lines earlier).
Ceiling: a no-op for a client whose profile already asks for one
segment.
Drop when: upstream adds its own fast-start, or a client stalls on a
one-entry playlist.

Patch: 0012
---
 Jellyfin.Api/Controllers/DynamicHlsController.cs | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Jellyfin.Api/Controllers/DynamicHlsController.cs b/Jellyfin.Api/Controllers/DynamicHlsController.cs
index 034a9de..44edddf 100644
--- a/Jellyfin.Api/Controllers/DynamicHlsController.cs
+++ b/Jellyfin.Api/Controllers/DynamicHlsController.cs
@@ -323,6 +323,17 @@ public class DynamicHlsController : BaseJellyfinApiController
                     }
 
                     minSegments = state.MinSegments;
+
+                    // Return the playlist as soon as ONE segment exists instead of the
+                    // client-requested two or three: the player starts fetching (and its
+                    // own buffering logic takes over) one-to-two segment durations sooner,
+                    // and ffmpeg keeps producing regardless. Serving a growing playlist is
+                    // ordinary HLS; hls.js (web/webOS) and ExoPlayer (Fire TV) both poll.
+                    if (minSegments > 1)
+                    {
+                        minSegments = 1;
+                    }
+
                     if (minSegments > 0)
                     {
                         await HlsHelpers.WaitForMinimumSegmentCount(playlistPath, minSegments, _logger, cancellationToken).ConfigureAwait(false);
